{
  "id": "lesson-linq-012",
  "slug": "thenby-thenbydescending",
  "moduleSlug": "sorting-grouping",
  "title": "Secondary Sorting with ThenBy & ThenByDescending",
  "description": "Learn to apply secondary sorting using ThenBy and ThenByDescending in method syntax.",
  "order": 12,
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedMinutes": 10,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "thenby",
    "thenbydescending",
    "sorting"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `ThenBy` and `ThenByDescending` extension methods are used for sorting on multiple fields in method syntax."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "ThenBy Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `OrderBy()` method sorts the collection in ascending order based on a specified field. Use `ThenBy()` after `OrderBy` to sort the collection on another field in ascending order."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "LINQ first sorts the collection based on the primary field (specified by `OrderBy`) and then sorts the result again based on the secondary field (specified by `ThenBy`)."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "ThenBy Example",
        "content": "Secondary sorting with ThenBy",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Steve\", Age = 15 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 25 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 20 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 19 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 6, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 18 }\n};\n\n// Sort by name, then by age (ascending)\nvar thenByResult = studentList.OrderBy(s => s.StudentName)\n                              .ThenBy(s => s.Age);\n\n// Result:\n// Bill, 25\n// John, 18\n// Ram, 18\n// Ram, 20\n// Ron, 19\n// Steve, 15"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "ThenByDescending Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Use `ThenByDescending` to apply secondary sorting in descending order:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "ThenByDescending Example",
        "content": "Secondary sorting in descending order",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "// Sort by name, then by age (descending)\nvar thenByDescResult = studentList.OrderBy(s => s.StudentName)\n                                   .ThenByDescending(s => s.Age);\n\n// Result:\n// Bill, 25\n// John, 18\n// Ram, 20  <- Note: 20 comes before 18\n// Ram, 18\n// Ron, 19\n// Steve, 15"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "info",
        "title": "Multiple ThenBy",
        "text": "You can chain multiple ThenBy/ThenByDescending calls for sorting on more than two fields."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Multiple ThenBy",
        "content": "Sorting on three or more fields",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "// Sort by name, then by age descending, then by ID\nvar result = studentList.OrderBy(s => s.StudentName)\n                        .ThenByDescending(s => s.Age)\n                        .ThenBy(s => s.StudentID);"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "ThenBy applies secondary sorting in ascending order",
          "ThenByDescending applies secondary sorting in descending order",
          "Must be used after OrderBy or OrderByDescending",
          "Multiple ThenBy calls can be chained for complex sorting"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to learn about grouping operators."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "orderby-orderbydescending",
    "grouping-operator-groupby-tolookup"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-013",
  "slug": "grouping-operator-groupby-tolookup",
  "moduleSlug": "sorting-grouping",
  "title": "Grouping with GroupBy & ToLookup",
  "description": "Learn to group elements by key values using GroupBy and create lookups with ToLookup.",
  "order": 13,
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedMinutes": 20,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "groupby",
    "tolookup",
    "grouping",
    "igrouping"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Grouping operators create groups of elements based on a given key, similar to the GROUP BY clause in SQL."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "data": {
        "headers": [
          "Grouping Operator",
          "Description"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "GroupBy",
            "Returns groups of elements based on a key value. Each group is an IGrouping<TKey, TElement> object."
          ],
          [
            "ToLookup",
            "Same as GroupBy, but with immediate execution. Only available in method syntax."
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "GroupBy Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `GroupBy` operator returns groups of elements from a collection based on a key value. Each group is represented by an `IGrouping<TKey, TElement>` object with a `.Key` property and an inner collection of elements."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 3,
        "text": "GroupBy in Query Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "GroupBy in Query Syntax",
        "content": "Grouping students by age",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Steve\", Age = 21 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 20 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Abram\", Age = 21 }\n};\n\nvar groupedResult = from s in studentList\n                    group s by s.Age;\n\n// Iterate each group\nforeach (var ageGroup in groupedResult)\n{\n    Console.WriteLine(\"Age Group: {0}\", ageGroup.Key);\n    foreach (Student s in ageGroup)\n        Console.WriteLine(\"Student Name: {0}\", s.StudentName);\n}\n\n// Output:\n// Age Group: 18\n// Student Name: John\n// Student Name: Bill\n// Age Group: 21\n// Student Name: Steve\n// Student Name: Abram\n// Age Group: 20\n// Student Name: Ram"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 3,
        "text": "GroupBy in Method Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "GroupBy in Method Syntax",
        "content": "Grouping with method syntax",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "var groupedResult = studentList.GroupBy(s => s.Age);\n\nforeach (var ageGroup in groupedResult)\n{\n    Console.WriteLine(\"Age Group: {0}\", ageGroup.Key);\n    foreach (Student s in ageGroup)\n        Console.WriteLine(\"Student Name: {0}\", s.StudentName);\n}"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "ToLookup Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`ToLookup` is the same as `GroupBy`, with one key difference: `GroupBy` uses **deferred execution**, while `ToLookup` uses **immediate execution**."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "warning",
        "title": "Query Syntax Not Supported",
        "text": "ToLookup is only available in method syntax and is not supported in query syntax."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "ToLookup Example",
        "content": "Creating a lookup with ToLookup",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Steve\", Age = 21 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 20 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Abram\", Age = 21 }\n};\n\nvar lookupResult = studentList.ToLookup(s => s.Age);\n\nforeach (var group in lookupResult)\n{\n    Console.WriteLine(\"Age Group: {0}\", group.Key);\n    foreach (Student s in group)\n        Console.WriteLine(\"Student Name: {0}\", s.StudentName);\n}\n\n// Access a specific group\nvar age18Group = lookupResult[18];"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
        "id": "groupby-tolookup-comparison",
        "caption": "GroupBy vs ToLookup execution",
        "definition": "graph LR\n    A[GroupBy] --> B[Deferred Execution]\n    B --> C[Query executes when iterated]\n    D[ToLookup] --> E[Immediate Execution]\n    E --> F[Query executes immediately]"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "key-terms",
      "data": {
        "terms": [
          {
            "term": "IGrouping<TKey, TElement>",
            "definition": "Represents a collection of objects that share a common key. Has a Key property and can be iterated."
          },
          {
            "term": "GroupBy",
            "definition": "Groups elements by a key with deferred execution. Available in query and method syntax."
          },
          {
            "term": "ToLookup",
            "definition": "Groups elements by a key with immediate execution. Only available in method syntax."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "GroupBy creates groups of elements based on a key",
          "Each group has a Key property and inner collection",
          "GroupBy uses deferred execution",
          "ToLookup uses immediate execution and is method syntax only"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next module to learn about joining operators."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "orderby-orderbydescending",
    "thenby-thenbydescending"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-011",
  "slug": "orderby-orderbydescending",
  "moduleSlug": "sorting-grouping",
  "title": "Sorting with OrderBy & OrderByDescending",
  "description": "Learn to sort collections in ascending and descending order using OrderBy and OrderByDescending.",
  "order": 11,
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedMinutes": 15,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "orderby",
    "orderbydescending",
    "sorting"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Sorting operators arrange the elements of a collection in ascending or descending order. LINQ includes several sorting operators:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "data": {
        "headers": [
          "Sorting Operator",
          "Description"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "OrderBy",
            "Sorts elements in ascending or descending order"
          ],
          [
            "OrderByDescending",
            "Sorts elements in descending order (method syntax only)"
          ],
          [
            "ThenBy",
            "Secondary sorting in ascending order (method syntax only)"
          ],
          [
            "ThenByDescending",
            "Secondary sorting in descending order (method syntax only)"
          ],
          [
            "Reverse",
            "Reverses the order of elements (method syntax only)"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "OrderBy Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`OrderBy` sorts the values of a collection in ascending order by default. Use the `descending` keyword to sort in descending order."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "OrderBy in Query Syntax",
        "content": "Sorting with OrderBy in query syntax",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Steve\", Age = 15 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 25 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 20 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 19 }\n};\n\n// Ascending order (default)\nvar orderByResult = from s in studentList\n                    orderby s.StudentName\n                    select s;\n\n// Result: Bill, John, Ram, Ron, Steve\n\n// Descending order\nvar orderByDescendingResult = from s in studentList\n                             orderby s.StudentName descending\n                             select s;\n\n// Result: Steve, Ron, Ram, John, Bill"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "OrderBy in Method Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `OrderBy` extension method has two overloads:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "OrderBy Method Signatures",
        "content": "The two overloads of OrderBy",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "// Overload 1: With key selector\npublic static IOrderedEnumerable<TSource> OrderBy<TSource, TKey>(\n    this IEnumerable<TSource> source,\n    Func<TSource, TKey> keySelector);\n\n// Overload 2: With key selector and custom comparer\npublic static IOrderedEnumerable<TSource> OrderBy<TSource, TKey>(\n    this IEnumerable<TSource> source,\n    Func<TSource, TKey> keySelector,\n    IComparer<TKey> comparer);"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "OrderBy in Method Syntax",
        "content": "Sorting with OrderBy in method syntax",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "var studentsInAscOrder = studentList.OrderBy(s => s.StudentName);"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "OrderByDescending Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`OrderByDescending` sorts the collection in descending order. It is **only valid in method syntax** and is not supported in query syntax."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "OrderByDescending",
        "content": "Sorting in descending order",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "var studentsInDescOrder = studentList.OrderByDescending(s => s.StudentName);\n\n// Result: Steve, Ron, Ram, John, Bill"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Multiple Sorting"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "You can sort on multiple fields separated by commas in query syntax. The collection is first sorted by the first field, and if values are equal, it uses the second field for sorting:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Multiple Sorting in Query Syntax",
        "content": "Sorting by multiple fields",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Steve\", Age = 15 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 25 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 20 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 19 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 6, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 18 }\n};\n\nvar orderByResult = from s in studentList\n                    orderby s.StudentName, s.Age\n                    select new { s.StudentName, s.Age };\n\n// Result:\n// Bill, 25\n// John, 18\n// Ram, 18\n// Ram, 20\n// Ron, 19\n// Steve, 15"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "info",
        "title": "Multiple Sorting in Method Syntax",
        "text": "In method syntax, use `ThenBy` or `ThenByDescending` for secondary sorting after `OrderBy`."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "OrderBy sorts in ascending order by default",
          "Use 'descending' keyword in query syntax for descending order",
          "OrderByDescending is only valid in method syntax",
          "Multiple sorting uses comma-separated fields in query syntax"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to learn about secondary sorting with ThenBy and ThenByDescending."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "thenby-thenbydescending",
    "grouping-operator-groupby-tolookup"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-020",
  "slug": "concat-concatenation-operator",
  "moduleSlug": "set-operations",
  "title": "Concat — Concatenation",
  "description": "Learn to append two sequences together using the Concat operator.",
  "order": 20,
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedMinutes": 10,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "concat",
    "concatenation",
    "append"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Concat` method appends two sequences of the same type and returns a new sequence (collection)."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Concat Example",
        "content": "Appending two collections together",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<string> collection1 = new List<string>() { \"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\" };\nIList<string> collection2 = new List<string>() { \"Five\", \"Six\" };\n\nvar collection3 = collection1.Concat(collection2);\n\n// Result: \"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\", \"Five\", \"Six\"\n\nIList<int> intCollection1 = new List<int>() { 1, 2, 3 };\nIList<int> intCollection2 = new List<int>() { 4, 5, 6 };\n\nvar intCollection3 = intCollection1.Concat(intCollection2);\n\n// Result: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "info",
        "title": "Concat vs Union",
        "text": "Concat appends all elements including duplicates. Use Union if you want to remove duplicates."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "warning",
        "title": "Not Supported in Query Syntax",
        "text": "The Concat operator is not supported in query syntax in C# or VB.NET."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Concat appends two sequences together",
          "Returns all elements including duplicates",
          "Both sequences must be of the same type",
          "Not supported in query syntax"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next module to learn about aggregation operators."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "distinct-set-operator",
    "union-set-operator"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-018",
  "slug": "intersect-set-operator",
  "moduleSlug": "set-operations",
  "title": "Intersect — Set Intersection",
  "description": "Learn to find common elements between two collections using Intersect.",
  "order": 18,
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedMinutes": 10,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "intersect",
    "set operator",
    "intersection"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Intersect` extension method requires two collections. It returns a new collection that includes common elements that exist in **both** collections."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Intersect with Primitive Types",
        "content": "Finding common elements between two collections",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<string> strList1 = new List<string>() { \"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\", \"Four\", \"Five\" };\nIList<string> strList2 = new List<string>() { \"Four\", \"Five\", \"Six\", \"Seven\", \"Eight\" };\n\nvar result = strList1.Intersect(strList2);\n\n// Result: \"Four\", \"Five\""
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Intersect with Complex Types"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "For complex types, you need to implement `IEqualityComparer<T>`:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Intersect with Custom Comparer",
        "content": "Finding common students between two lists",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList1 = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Steve\", Age = 15 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 25 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 19 }\n};\n\nIList<Student> studentList2 = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 25 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 19 }\n};\n\nvar result = studentList1.Intersect(studentList2, new StudentComparer());\n\n// Result: Bill, Ron"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "warning",
        "title": "Not Supported in Query Syntax",
        "text": "The Intersect operator is not supported in C# or VB.NET query syntax. Use method syntax only."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Intersect returns elements common to both collections",
          "It's a set intersection operation",
          "Requires IEqualityComparer<T> for complex types",
          "Not supported in query syntax"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to learn about the Union operator."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "distinct-set-operator",
    "except-set-operator",
    "union-set-operator"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-019",
  "slug": "union-set-operator",
  "moduleSlug": "set-operations",
  "title": "Union — Combining Sets",
  "description": "Learn to combine two collections into one with distinct elements using Union.",
  "order": 19,
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedMinutes": 10,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "union",
    "set operator",
    "combine"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Union` extension method requires two collections and returns a new collection that includes **distinct** elements from both collections combined."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Union with Primitive Types",
        "content": "Combining two collections with distinct elements",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<string> strList1 = new List<string>() { \"One\", \"Two\", \"three\", \"Four\" };\nIList<string> strList2 = new List<string>() { \"Two\", \"THREE\", \"Four\", \"Five\" };\n\nvar result = strList1.Union(strList2);\n\n// Result: \"One\", \"Two\", \"three\", \"Four\", \"THREE\", \"Five\"\n// Note: Case-sensitive distinct (three != THREE)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Union with Complex Types"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "For complex types, you need to implement `IEqualityComparer<T>`:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Union with Custom Comparer",
        "content": "Combining student lists with distinct elements",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList1 = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Steve\", Age = 15 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 25 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 19 }\n};\n\nIList<Student> studentList2 = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 25 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 19 }\n};\n\nvar result = studentList1.Union(studentList2, new StudentComparer());\n\n// Result: John, Steve, Bill, Ron (duplicates removed)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "warning",
        "title": "Not Supported in Query Syntax",
        "text": "The Union operator is not supported in C# or VB.NET query syntax. Use method syntax only."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Union combines two collections into one with distinct elements",
          "Automatically removes duplicates",
          "Requires IEqualityComparer<T> for complex types",
          "Not supported in query syntax"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to learn about the Concat operator."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "distinct-set-operator",
    "except-set-operator",
    "intersect-set-operator"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-016",
  "slug": "distinct-set-operator",
  "moduleSlug": "set-operations",
  "title": "Distinct — Removing Duplicates",
  "description": "Learn to remove duplicate elements from collections using the Distinct operator.",
  "order": 16,
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedMinutes": 10,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "distinct",
    "set operator",
    "unique"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Distinct` extension method returns a new collection of unique elements from the given collection, removing any duplicates."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Distinct with Primitive Types",
        "content": "Removing duplicates from primitive type collections",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<string> strList = new List<string>() { \"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\", \"Two\", \"Three\" };\nIList<int> intList = new List<int>() { 1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 4, 3, 5 };\n\nvar distinctList1 = strList.Distinct();\n// Result: \"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\"\n\nvar distinctList2 = intList.Distinct();\n// Result: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Distinct with Complex Types"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Distinct` method doesn't compare values of complex type objects by default. You need to implement `IEqualityComparer<T>` to compare values of complex types:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Distinct with Custom Comparer",
        "content": "Removing duplicates from complex type collections",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "public class Student\n{\n    public int StudentID { get; set; }\n    public string StudentName { get; set; }\n    public int Age { get; set; }\n}\n\nclass StudentComparer : IEqualityComparer<Student>\n{\n    public bool Equals(Student x, Student y)\n    {\n        if (x.StudentID == y.StudentID &&\n            x.StudentName.ToLower() == y.StudentName.ToLower())\n            return true;\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    public int GetHashCode(Student obj)\n    {\n        return obj.StudentID.GetHashCode();\n    }\n}\n\nIList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Steve\", Age = 15 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 25 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 25 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 19 }\n};\n\nvar distinctStudents = studentList.Distinct(new StudentComparer());\n// Result: John, Steve, Bill, Ron (duplicates removed)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "warning",
        "title": "Not Supported in Query Syntax",
        "text": "The Distinct operator is not supported in C# query syntax. Use method syntax or call Distinct() on a query variable."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Distinct removes duplicate elements from a collection",
          "Works directly with primitive types",
          "Requires IEqualityComparer<T> for complex types",
          "Not supported in C# query syntax"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to learn about the Except operator."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "except-set-operator",
    "intersect-set-operator",
    "union-set-operator"
  ]
}
{
