{
  "id": "lesson-linq-008",
  "slug": "filtering-operator-where",
  "moduleSlug": "filtering-projection",
  "title": "Filtering with Where",
  "description": "Learn to filter collections using the Where operator with predicates and lambda expressions.",
  "order": 8,
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedMinutes": 15,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "where",
    "filtering",
    "predicate"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Filtering operators in LINQ filter a sequence (collection) based on specified criteria. The most commonly used filtering operator is **Where**."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "The Where Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Where` extension method filters a collection based on a given criteria expression and returns a new collection. The criteria can be specified as a lambda expression or `Func` delegate."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Where` extension method has two overloads:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Where Method Signatures",
        "content": "The two overloads of the Where method",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "// Overload 1: Predicate without index\npublic static IEnumerable<TSource> Where<TSource>(\n    this IEnumerable<TSource> source,\n    Func<TSource, bool> predicate);\n\n// Overload 2: Predicate with index\npublic static IEnumerable<TSource> Where<TSource>(\n    this IEnumerable<TSource> source,\n    Func<TSource, int, bool> predicate);"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Where in Query Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The following query uses the `Where` operator to filter teenage students from a collection:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Where in Query Syntax",
        "content": "Filtering with Where in query syntax",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 13 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Moin\", Age = 21 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 20 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 15 }\n};\n\nvar filteredResult = from s in studentList\n                     where s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20\n                     select s.StudentName;\n\n// Result: John, Bill, Ron"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "In this example, the lambda expression `s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20` is passed as a predicate function `Func<Student, bool>` that evaluates every student in the collection."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Where in Method Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "In method syntax, you pass the entire lambda expression as a predicate function:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Where in Method Syntax",
        "content": "Filtering with Where in method syntax",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "var filteredResult = studentList.Where(s => s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20);"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Using Named Methods as Predicates"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "You can also use a named method as a predicate function:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Named Method as Predicate",
        "content": "Using a named method with Where",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "public static bool IsTeenAger(Student stud)\n{\n    return stud.Age > 12 && stud.Age < 20;\n}\n\n// In query syntax\nvar filteredResult = from s in studentList\n                     where IsTeenAger(s)\n                     select s;\n\n// In method syntax\nvar filteredResult = studentList.Where(IsTeenAger);"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Where with Index"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The second overload of `Where` includes the index of the current element. You can use this index in your logic:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Where with Index",
        "content": "Filtering elements at even indices",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Steve\", Age = 15 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 25 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 20 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 19 }\n};\n\nvar filteredResult = studentList.Where((s, i) => {\n    if (i % 2 == 0) // if it is even element (index 0, 2, 4)\n        return true;\n    return false;\n});\n\n// Result: John, Bill, Ron (elements at index 0, 2, 4)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Multiple Where Clauses"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "You can chain multiple `Where` clauses to apply additional filtering:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Multiple Where Clauses",
        "content": "Chaining multiple Where clauses",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "// Query syntax\nvar filteredResult = from s in studentList\n                     where s.Age > 12\n                     where s.Age < 20\n                     select s;\n\n// Method syntax\nvar filteredResult = studentList\n    .Where(s => s.Age > 12)\n    .Where(s => s.Age < 20);"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Where filters collections based on a predicate function",
          "Two overloads: with and without index parameter",
          "Can use lambda expressions, anonymous methods, or named methods as predicates",
          "Multiple Where clauses can be chained for complex filtering"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to learn about the OfType filtering operator."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "oftype-filtering-operator",
    "projection-operators"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-010",
  "slug": "projection-operators",
  "moduleSlug": "filtering-projection",
  "title": "Projection Operators (Select & SelectMany)",
  "description": "Learn to transform data using Select and flatten nested collections with SelectMany.",
  "order": 10,
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedMinutes": 15,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "select",
    "selectmany",
    "projection",
    "anonymous types"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Projection operators transform elements in a collection into a new shape. LINQ provides two projection operators: **Select** and **SelectMany**."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Select Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Select` operator always returns an `IEnumerable` collection containing elements based on a transformation function. It is similar to the SELECT clause in SQL that produces a flat result set."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Select in Query Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "LINQ query syntax must end with a `Select` or `GroupBy` clause. The following example demonstrates the `Select` operator returning a collection of student names:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Select in Query Syntax",
        "content": "Selecting a single property",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\" },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Moin\" },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\" },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\" },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\" }\n};\n\nvar selectResult = from s in studentList\n                   select s.StudentName;\n\n// Result: \"John\", \"Moin\", \"Bill\", \"Ram\", \"Ron\""
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Select with Anonymous Types"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Select` operator can return a collection of custom class or anonymous type instances:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Select with Anonymous Types",
        "content": "Creating anonymous objects with Select",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 13 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Moin\", Age = 21 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 20 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 15 }\n};\n\n// Returns collection of anonymous objects with Name and Age\nvar selectResult = from s in studentList\n                   select new { Name = \"Mr. \" + s.StudentName, Age = s.Age };\n\nforeach (var item in selectResult)\n    Console.WriteLine($\"Student Name: {item.Name}, Age: {item.Age}\");\n\n// Output:\n// Student Name: Mr. John, Age: 13\n// Student Name: Mr. Moin, Age: 21\n// Student Name: Mr. Bill, Age: 18\n// Student Name: Mr. Ram, Age: 20\n// Student Name: Mr. Ron, Age: 15"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Select in Method Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Select` operator is optional in method syntax, but you can use it to shape the data:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Select in Method Syntax",
        "content": "Using Select with method syntax",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Moin\", Age = 21 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 20 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 21 }\n};\n\nvar selectResult = studentList.Select(s => new { Name = s.StudentName, Age = s.Age });"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "SelectMany Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `SelectMany` operator projects sequences of values based on a transform function and then flattens them into one sequence. This is useful for working with nested collections."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "SelectMany Example",
        "content": "Flattening nested collections with SelectMany",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "public class Student\n{\n    public string Name { get; set; }\n    public List<string> Courses { get; set; }\n}\n\nList<Student> students = new List<Student>\n{\n    new Student { Name = \"John\", Courses = new List<string> { \"Math\", \"Science\" } },\n    new Student { Name = \"Jane\", Courses = new List<string> { \"English\", \"History\" } }\n};\n\n// SelectMany flattens the nested collections\nvar allCourses = students.SelectMany(s => s.Courses);\n\n// Result: \"Math\", \"Science\", \"English\", \"History\"\n\n// SelectMany with student and course\nvar studentCourses = students.SelectMany(\n    s => s.Courses,\n    (student, course) => new { student.Name, course }\n);\n\n// Result:\n// { Name = John, course = Math }\n// { Name = John, course = Science }\n// { Name = Jane, course = English }\n// { Name = Jane, course = History }"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
        "id": "select-vs-selectmany",
        "caption": "Select vs SelectMany behavior",
        "definition": "graph LR\n    A[Select] --> B[Transforms each element]\n    B --> C[Returns same number of elements]\n    D[SelectMany] --> E[Transforms and flattens]\n    E --> F[Returns flattened sequence]"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "key-terms",
      "data": {
        "terms": [
          {
            "term": "Select",
            "definition": "Transforms each element into a new shape. Returns one output element per input element."
          },
          {
            "term": "SelectMany",
            "definition": "Transforms each element into a sequence and flattens all sequences into one."
          },
          {
            "term": "Anonymous Type",
            "definition": "A type created on the fly with read-only properties, often used with Select for projections."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Select transforms each element into a new shape",
          "Select can return anonymous types with custom properties",
          "SelectMany flattens nested collections into a single sequence",
          "Both operators are available in query and method syntax"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next module to learn about sorting and grouping operators."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "filtering-operator-where",
    "oftype-filtering-operator"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-009",
  "slug": "oftype-filtering-operator",
  "moduleSlug": "filtering-projection",
  "title": "OfType Filtering Operator",
  "description": "Learn to filter collections by type using the OfType operator.",
  "order": 9,
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedMinutes": 10,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "oftype",
    "filtering",
    "type"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `OfType` operator filters a collection based on the ability to cast an element to a specified type. This is particularly useful when working with mixed-type collections like `ArrayList`."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "OfType Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `OfType<TResult>()` extension method returns only the elements that can be successfully cast to the specified type `TResult`. Elements that cannot be cast are excluded from the result."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "OfType Example",
        "content": "Filtering mixed-type collections with OfType",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList mixedList = new ArrayList();\nmixedList.Add(0);\nmixedList.Add(\"One\");\nmixedList.Add(\"Two\");\nmixedList.Add(3);\nmixedList.Add(new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"Bill\" });\n\n// Get only strings\nvar stringResult = from s in mixedList.OfType<string>()\n                   select s;\n\n// stringResult: \"One\", \"Two\"\n\n// Get only integers\nvar intResult = from s in mixedList.OfType<int>()\n                select s;\n\n// intResult: 0, 3"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "OfType in Method Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "You can also use `OfType` in method syntax:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "OfType in Method Syntax",
        "content": "Using OfType with method syntax",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "var stringResult = mixedList.OfType<string>();\nvar intResult = mixedList.OfType<int>();\nvar studentResult = mixedList.OfType<Student>();"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "tip",
        "title": "When to Use OfType",
        "text": "Use OfType when working with non-generic collections (like ArrayList) that contain elements of different types. For generic collections, use Where with type checking instead."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "OfType filters collections by type",
          "Returns only elements that can be cast to the specified type",
          "Useful for mixed-type collections like ArrayList",
          "Available in both query and method syntax"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to learn about projection operators (Select and SelectMany)."
      }
    }
