{
  "id": "lesson-linq-015",
  "slug": "groupjoin-operator",
  "moduleSlug": "joining-data",
  "title": "Group Join with GroupJoin",
  "description": "Learn to perform left outer joins using the GroupJoin operator.",
  "order": 15,
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedMinutes": 20,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "groupjoin",
    "left outer join",
    "joining"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `GroupJoin` operator joins two sequences based on keys and groups the results. It is equivalent to a **left outer join** in SQL."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "GroupJoin in Method Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`GroupJoin` requires the same parameters as `Join`. The key difference is that the result selector receives an `IEnumerable<TInner>` for the grouped inner sequence:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "GroupJoin Method Signature",
        "content": "The GroupJoin method signature",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "public static IEnumerable<TResult> GroupJoin<TOuter, TInner, TKey, TResult>(\n    this IEnumerable<TOuter> outer,\n    IEnumerable<TInner> inner,\n    Func<TOuter, TKey> outerKeySelector,\n    Func<TInner, TKey> innerKeySelector,\n    Func<TOuter, IEnumerable<TInner>, TResult> resultSelector);"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "GroupJoin in Method Syntax",
        "content": "Group joining standards with their students",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", StandardID = 1 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Moin\", StandardID = 1 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", StandardID = 2 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", StandardID = 2 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\" }\n};\n\nIList<Standard> standardList = new List<Standard>() {\n    new Standard() { StandardID = 1, StandardName = \"Standard 1\" },\n    new Standard() { StandardID = 2, StandardName = \"Standard 2\" },\n    new Standard() { StandardID = 3, StandardName = \"Standard 3\" }\n};\n\nvar groupJoin = standardList.GroupJoin(\n    studentList,                      // inner sequence\n    std => std.StandardID,            // outerKeySelector\n    s => s.StandardID,                // innerKeySelector\n    (std, studentsGroup) => new       // result selector\n    {\n        Students = studentsGroup,\n        StandardName = std.StandardName\n    });\n\nforeach (var item in groupJoin)\n{\n    Console.WriteLine(item.StandardName);\n    foreach (var stud in item.Students)\n        Console.WriteLine(\"  \" + stud.StudentName);\n}\n\n// Output:\n// Standard 1:\n//   John\n//   Moin\n// Standard 2:\n//   Bill\n//   Ram\n// Standard 3:\n//   (no students)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "info",
        "title": "Left Outer Join Behavior",
        "text": "GroupJoin includes all elements from the outer sequence, even if there are no matches in the inner sequence. The inner collection will be empty for unmatched outer elements."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "GroupJoin in Query Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "In query syntax, use the `into` keyword to create the grouped collection:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "GroupJoin in Query Syntax",
        "content": "Group joining with query syntax",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "var groupJoin = from std in standardList\n                 join s in studentList\n                 on std.StandardID equals s.StandardID\n                 into studentGroup\n                 select new\n                 {\n                     Students = studentGroup,\n                     StandardName = std.StandardName\n                 };\n\nforeach (var item in groupJoin)\n{\n    Console.WriteLine(item.StandardName);\n    foreach (var stud in item.Students)\n        Console.WriteLine(\"  \" + stud.StudentName);\n}"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
        "id": "groupjoin-diagram",
        "caption": "GroupJoin returns all outer elements with grouped inner matches",
        "definition": "graph LR\n    A[Outer Sequence] --> C[GroupJoin Result]\n    B[Inner Sequence] --> C\n    C --> D[All outer elements included]\n    C --> D"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "key-terms",
      "data": {
        "terms": [
          {
            "term": "Join",
            "definition": "Inner join - returns only elements with matches in both sequences."
          },
          {
            "term": "GroupJoin",
            "definition": "Left outer join - returns all outer elements with grouped inner matches."
          },
          {
            "term": "into keyword",
            "definition": "In query syntax, creates the grouped collection for GroupJoin."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "GroupJoin performs a left outer join",
          "All elements from the outer sequence are included",
          "Inner elements are grouped into a collection",
          "Use 'into' keyword in query syntax"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next module to learn about set operations."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "join-operator"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-014",
  "slug": "join-operator",
  "moduleSlug": "joining-data",
  "title": "Inner Join with Join",
  "description": "Learn to combine two collections using the Join operator, equivalent to SQL inner join.",
  "order": 14,
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedMinutes": 20,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "join",
    "inner join",
    "joining"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Join` operator combines two sequences (collections) based on matching keys and returns a result. It is equivalent to an **inner join** in SQL."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Join in Method Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Join` extension method has two overloads. The first overload takes five parameters:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Join Method Signature",
        "content": "The Join method overloads",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "public static IEnumerable<TResult> Join<TOuter, TInner, TKey, TResult>(\n    this IEnumerable<TOuter> outer,\n    IEnumerable<TInner> inner,\n    Func<TOuter, TKey> outerKeySelector,\n    Func<TInner, TKey> innerKeySelector,\n    Func<TOuter, TInner, TResult> resultSelector);\n\n// Overload with custom comparer\npublic static IEnumerable<TResult> Join<TOuter, TInner, TKey, TResult>(\n    this IEnumerable<TOuter> outer,\n    IEnumerable<TInner> inner,\n    Func<TOuter, TKey> outerKeySelector,\n    Func<TInner, TKey> innerKeySelector,\n    Func<TOuter, TInner, TResult> resultSelector,\n    IEqualityComparer<TKey> comparer);"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The five parameters are:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "**outer** — The first (outer) sequence",
          "**inner** — The second (inner) sequence",
          "**outerKeySelector** — Lambda to extract the key from outer sequence",
          "**innerKeySelector** — Lambda to extract the key from inner sequence",
          "**resultSelector** — Lambda to create the result from matching elements"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Join in Method Syntax",
        "content": "Joining students with their standards",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "public class Student\n{\n    public int StudentID { get; set; }\n    public string StudentName { get; set; }\n    public int StandardID { get; set; }\n}\n\npublic class Standard\n{\n    public int StandardID { get; set; }\n    public string StandardName { get; set; }\n}\n\nIList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", StandardID = 1 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Moin\", StandardID = 1 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", StandardID = 2 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", StandardID = 2 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\" }\n};\n\nIList<Standard> standardList = new List<Standard>() {\n    new Standard() { StandardID = 1, StandardName = \"Standard 1\" },\n    new Standard() { StandardID = 2, StandardName = \"Standard 2\" },\n    new Standard() { StandardID = 3, StandardName = \"Standard 3\" }\n};\n\nvar innerJoin = studentList.Join(\n    standardList,                    // inner sequence\n    student => student.StandardID,   // outerKeySelector\n    standard => standard.StandardID, // innerKeySelector\n    (student, standard) => new       // result selector\n    {\n        StudentName = student.StudentName,\n        StandardName = standard.StandardName\n    });\n\n// Result:\n// John - Standard 1\n// Moin - Standard 1\n// Bill - Standard 2\n// Ram - Standard 2\n// Note: Ron is not included (no matching StandardID)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "warning",
        "title": "Inner Join Behavior",
        "text": "Only elements with matching keys in both sequences are included in the result. Elements without matches are excluded."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Join in Query Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "In query syntax, the `Join` operator uses the `on` and `equals` keywords:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Join in Query Syntax",
        "content": "Joining with query syntax",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "var innerJoin = from s in studentList          // outer sequence\n                 join st in standardList          // inner sequence\n                 on s.StandardID equals st.StandardID // key selector\n                 select new                       // result selector\n                 {\n                     StudentName = s.StudentName,\n                     StandardName = st.StandardName\n                 };\n\n// Result:\n// John - Standard 1\n// Moin - Standard 1\n// Bill - Standard 2\n// Ram - Standard 2"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "important",
        "title": "Use 'equals' not '=='",
        "text": "In query syntax, use the 'equals' keyword to match keys, not the '==' operator."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
        "id": "inner-join-diagram",
        "caption": "Inner join returns only matching elements from both collections",
        "definition": "graph LR\n    A[Student List] --> C[Join Result]\n    B[Standard List] --> C\n    C --> D[Only matching keys included]"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Join combines two sequences based on matching keys",
          "Equivalent to SQL inner join",
          "Only elements with matches in both sequences are included",
          "Use 'equals' keyword in query syntax, not '=='"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to learn about GroupJoin for left outer joins."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "groupjoin-operator"
  ]
}
