{
  "id": "lesson-linq-038",
  "slug": "sequenceequal-operator",
  "moduleSlug": "advanced-linq-concepts",
  "title": "SequenceEqual — LINQ Equality Operator",
  "description": "Learn to compare sequences for equality using the SequenceEqual operator.",
  "order": 38,
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "estimatedMinutes": 10,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "sequenceequal",
    "equality",
    "comparison"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `SequenceEqual` method checks whether two sequences are equal by comparing the number of elements, the value of each element, and the order of elements."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "SequenceEqual with Primitive Types"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "SequenceEqual Examples",
        "content": "Comparing sequences of primitive types",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<string> strList1 = new List<string>() { \"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\", \"Four\", \"Three\" };\nIList<string> strList2 = new List<string>() { \"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\", \"Four\", \"Three\" };\nIList<string> strList3 = new List<string>() { \"Two\", \"One\", \"Three\", \"Four\", \"Three\" };\n\nbool isEqual1 = strList1.SequenceEqual(strList2); // true (same order)\nbool isEqual2 = strList1.SequenceEqual(strList3); // false (different order)\n\nConsole.WriteLine(isEqual1); // True\nConsole.WriteLine(isEqual2); // False"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "SequenceEqual with Complex Types"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "For complex types, `SequenceEqual` compares references by default, not values:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "SequenceEqual with Complex Types",
        "content": "Reference comparison with complex types",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "Student std = new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"Bill\" };\n\nIList<Student> studentList1 = new List<Student>() { std };\nIList<Student> studentList2 = new List<Student>() { std };\n\nbool isEqual1 = studentList1.SequenceEqual(studentList2); // true (same reference)\n\nStudent std1 = new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"Bill\" };\nStudent std2 = new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"Bill\" };\n\nIList<Student> studentList3 = new List<Student>() { std1 };\nIList<Student> studentList4 = new List<Student>() { std2 };\n\nbool isEqual2 = studentList3.SequenceEqual(studentList4); // false (different references)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "To compare values of complex types, implement `IEqualityComparer<T>`:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "SequenceEqual with Custom Comparer",
        "content": "Value comparison with IEqualityComparer",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "class StudentComparer : IEqualityComparer<Student>\n{\n    public bool Equals(Student x, Student y)\n    {\n        if (x.StudentID == y.StudentID &&\n            x.StudentName.ToLower() == y.StudentName.ToLower())\n            return true;\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    public int GetHashCode(Student obj)\n    {\n        return obj.GetHashCode();\n    }\n}\n\nbool isEqual = studentList1.SequenceEqual(studentList2, new StudentComparer());"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "warning",
        "title": "Not Supported in Query Syntax",
        "text": "The SequenceEqual operator is not supported in C# or VB.NET query syntax."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "SequenceEqual compares element count, values, and order",
          "For primitive types: compares values",
          "For complex types: compares references by default",
          "Use IEqualityComparer<T> for value comparison of complex types"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to explore sample LINQ queries."
      }
    }
  ],
