{
  "id": "lesson-linq-037",
  "slug": "expression-trees",
  "moduleSlug": "advanced-linq-concepts",
  "title": "Expression Trees",
  "description": "Understand expression trees and how they enable LINQ providers like Entity Framework.",
  "order": 37,
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "estimatedMinutes": 20,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "expression trees",
    "Expression<T>",
    "IQueryable"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "An expression tree is an in-memory representation of a lambda expression arranged in a tree-like data structure. Each node in an expression tree is an expression."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "What are Expression Trees?"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Expression trees hold the actual elements of a query, not the result of the query. They make the structure of lambda expressions transparent and explicit, allowing you to interact with the data just like any other data structure."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Expression Tree Example",
        "content": "Creating an expression tree from a lambda",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "Expression<Func<Student, bool>> isTeenAgerExpr = s => s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20;"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Building Expression Trees Manually"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "You can build expression trees manually using the `Expression` class:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Manual Expression Tree",
        "content": "Building an expression tree step by step",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "// Step 1: Create parameter expression\nParameterExpression pe = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Student), \"s\");\n\n// Step 2: Create property expression\nMemberExpression me = Expression.Property(pe, \"Age\");\n\n// Step 3: Create constant expression\nConstantExpression constant = Expression.Constant(18, typeof(int));\n\n// Step 4: Create binary expression\nBinaryExpression body = Expression.GreaterThanOrEqual(me, constant);\n\n// Step 5: Create lambda expression\nvar isAdultExprTree = Expression.Lambda<Func<Student, bool>>(body, new[] { pe });\n\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Expression Tree: {isAdultExprTree}\");\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Body: {isAdultExprTree.Body}\");\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Parameters: {isAdultExprTree.Parameters[0]}\");"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Inspecting Expression Trees"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Inspecting Expression Trees",
        "content": "Retrieving information from expression trees",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "Expression<Func<Student, bool>> isTeenAgerExpr = s => s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20;\n\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Expression: {isTeenAgerExpr}\");\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Expression Type: {isTeenAgerExpr.NodeType}\");\n\nvar parameters = isTeenAgerExpr.Parameters;\nforeach (var param in parameters)\n{\n    Console.WriteLine($\"Parameter Name: {param.Name}\");\n    Console.WriteLine($\"Parameter Type: {param.Type.Name}\");\n}\n\nvar bodyExpr = isTeenAgerExpr.Body as BinaryExpression;\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Left side: {bodyExpr.Left}\");\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Binary Expression Type: {bodyExpr.NodeType}\");\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Right side: {bodyExpr.Right}\");\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Return Type: {isTeenAgerExpr.ReturnType}\");"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Why Expression Trees?"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Lambda expressions assigned to `Func<T>` compile into executable code for in-memory collections. Lambda expressions assigned to `Expression<TDelegate>` compile into expression trees."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Expression trees are essential for remote LINQ providers like LINQ-to-SQL and Entity Framework. They can be translated into SQL queries that execute on the database server."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
        "id": "expression-tree-process",
        "caption": "How expression trees enable remote query providers",
        "definition": "graph LR\n    A[LINQ Query] --> B[Expression Tree]\n    B --> C[Queryable Extension Methods]\n    C --> D[Remote Provider]\n    D --> E[SQL Translation]\n    E --> F[Database Execution]"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Expression trees are in-memory representations of lambda expressions",
          "They hold query structure, not results",
          "Can be built manually using the Expression class",
          "Enable LINQ providers to translate queries to SQL"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to learn about SequenceEqual."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "sequenceequal-operator",
    "sample-linq-queries"
  ]
}
