{
  "id": "lesson-linq-025",
  "slug": "aggregation-aggregate",
  "moduleSlug": "aggregation-operators",
  "title": "Custom Aggregation with Aggregate",
  "description": "Learn to perform custom aggregation operations using the Aggregate operator.",
  "order": 25,
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedMinutes": 20,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "aggregate",
    "aggregation",
    "custom"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Aggregate` method performs custom accumulation operations on a collection. It has three overloads:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Aggregate Method Signatures",
        "content": "The three overloads of Aggregate",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "// Overload 1: Basic accumulation\npublic static TSource Aggregate<TSource>(\n    this IEnumerable<TSource> source,\n    Func<TSource, TSource, TSource> func);\n\n// Overload 2: With seed value\npublic static TAccumulate Aggregate<TSource, TAccumulate>(\n    this IEnumerable<TSource> source,\n    TAccumulate seed,\n    Func<TAccumulate, TSource, TAccumulate> func);\n\n// Overload 3: With seed and result selector\npublic static TResult Aggregate<TSource, TAccumulate, TResult>(\n    this IEnumerable<TSource> source,\n    TAccumulate seed,\n    Func<TAccumulate, TSource, TAccumulate> func,\n    Func<TAccumulate, TResult> resultSelector);"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Basic Aggregate"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Basic Aggregate",
        "content": "Creating a comma-separated string",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<string> strList = new List<string>() { \"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\", \"Four\", \"Five\" };\n\nvar commaSeparatedString = strList.Aggregate((s1, s2) => s1 + \", \" + s2);\n\n// Result: \"One, Two, Three, Four, Five\""
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Aggregate with Seed Value"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The second overload allows you to specify a seed value to start the accumulation:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Aggregate with Seed",
        "content": "Adding a prefix to the accumulation",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 13 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Moin\", Age = 21 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 20 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 15 }\n};\n\nstring commaSeparatedStudentNames = studentList.Aggregate<Student, string>(\n    \"Student Names: \",  // seed value\n    (str, s) => str += s.StudentName + \",\");\n\n// Result: \"Student Names: John, Moin, Bill, Ram, Ron,\""
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Aggregate with Result Selector"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The third overload allows you to specify a result selector to transform the final accumulation:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Aggregate with Result Selector",
        "content": "Removing the trailing comma",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "string commaSeparatedStudentNames = studentList.Aggregate<Student, string, string>(\n    String.Empty,                                    // seed value\n    (str, s) => str += s.StudentName + \",\",          // accumulation\n    str => str.Substring(0, str.Length - 1)          // result selector\n);\n\n// Result: \"John, Moin, Bill, Ram, Ron\""
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Aggregate for Sum",
        "content": "Using Aggregate to calculate sum",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "int sumOfStudentsAge = studentList.Aggregate<Student, int>(\n    0,\n    (totalAge, s) => totalAge += s.Age\n);\n\n// Result: 87"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "warning",
        "title": "Not Supported in Query Syntax",
        "text": "The Aggregate operator is not supported in query syntax in C# or VB.NET."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Aggregate performs custom accumulation operations",
          "Three overloads: basic, with seed, and with result selector",
          "Seed value provides a starting point for accumulation",
          "Result selector transforms the final result"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next module to learn about quantifiers and element operators."
      }
    }
  ],
