      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "ElementAtOrDefault Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `ElementAtOrDefault()` method returns an element at the specified index. If the index is out of range, it returns the default value of the data type instead of throwing an exception."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "ElementAtOrDefault Example",
        "content": "Retrieving elements safely with default values",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<int> intList = new List<int>() { 10, 21, 30, 45, 50, 87 };\nIList<string> strList = new List<string>() { \"One\", \"Two\", null, \"Four\", \"Five\" };\n\nConsole.WriteLine(intList.ElementAtOrDefault(9));  // 0 (default int)\nConsole.WriteLine(strList.ElementAtOrDefault(9));  // null (default string)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "tip",
        "title": "Safe Alternative",
        "text": "Use ElementAtOrDefault to avoid runtime exceptions when the index might be out of range."
