  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Quantifier operators evaluate elements of a sequence against a condition and return a boolean value indicating whether some or all elements satisfy the condition."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "All Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `All` operator returns `true` if **all** elements in the collection satisfy the specified condition."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "All Example",
        "content": "Checking if all students are teenagers",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Steve\", Age = 15 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 25 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 20 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 19 }\n};\n\n// Checks whether all the students are teenagers\nbool areAllStudentsTeenAger = studentList.All(s => s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20);\n\n// Result: false (Bill is 25, Ram is 20)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Any Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Any` operator returns `true` if **any** element in the collection satisfies the specified condition."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Any Example",
        "content": "Checking if any student is a teenager",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "bool anyTeenAger = studentList.Any(s => s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20);\n\n// Result: true (John, Steve, Ron are teenagers)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "warning",
        "title": "Not Supported in Query Syntax",
        "text": "Quantifier operators are not supported in C# query syntax. Use method syntax only."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "All returns true if all elements satisfy the condition",
          "Any returns true if any element satisfies the condition",
          "Both return boolean values",
          "Not supported in C# query syntax"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to learn about the Contains operator."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "contains-quantifier-operator",
    "first-firstordefault"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-031",
  "slug": "single-singleordefault",
  "moduleSlug": "quantifiers-element-operators",
  "title": "Single & SingleOrDefault",
  "description": "Learn to retrieve a single element from collections using Single and SingleOrDefault.",
  "order": 31,
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedMinutes": 10,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "single",
    "singleordefault",
    "element operators"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Single` and `SingleOrDefault` methods return a single element from a collection. They are stricter than `First` and `Last` — they throw an exception if the collection contains more than one element."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Single Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Single` method returns the only element from a collection. If the collection is empty or contains more than one element, it throws an `InvalidOperationException`."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Single Examples",
        "content": "Retrieving a single element",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<int> oneElementList = new List<int>() { 7 };\nIList<int> intList = new List<int>() { 7, 10, 21, 30, 45, 50, 87 };\n\nConsole.WriteLine(oneElementList.Single());                    // 7\nConsole.WriteLine(intList.Single(i => i < 10));              // 7 (only one < 10)\n\n// These throw exceptions:\n// intList.Single()                    // multiple elements\n// intList.Single(i => i < 100)        // multiple elements match\n// emptyList.Single()                  // no elements"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "SingleOrDefault Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `SingleOrDefault` method returns the only element, or the default value if the collection is empty. However, it still throws an exception if the collection contains more than one element."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "SingleOrDefault Examples",
        "content": "Retrieving a single element safely",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<int> oneElementList = new List<int>() { 7 };\nIList<int> intList = new List<int>() { 7, 10, 21, 30, 45, 50, 87 };\nIList<string> emptyList = new List<string>();\n\nConsole.WriteLine(oneElementList.SingleOrDefault());                    // 7\nConsole.WriteLine(emptyList.SingleOrDefault());                          // null\nConsole.WriteLine(intList.SingleOrDefault(i => i < 10));                // 7\n\n// These throw exceptions:\n// intList.SingleOrDefault(i => i < 100)  // multiple elements match"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "warning",
        "title": "Strict Behavior",
        "text": "Single and SingleOrDefault throw an exception if there are multiple matching elements. Use FirstOrDefault if you only need the first match."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Single returns the only element from a collection",
          "Single throws if collection is empty or has multiple elements",
          "SingleOrDefault returns default if empty, throws if multiple",
          "Useful when you expect exactly one match"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next module to learn about partitioning operators."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "first-firstordefault",
    "last-lastordefault"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-007",
  "slug": "anatomy-of-lambda-expression",
  "moduleSlug": "linq-fundamentals-syntax",
  "title": "Anatomy of the Lambda Expression",
  "description": "Understand lambda expressions, the building blocks of LINQ method syntax, and how to use them effectively.",
  "order": 7,
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedMinutes": 20,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "lambda",
    "expression",
    "func",
    "action",
    "delegate"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "C# 3.0 (.NET 3.5) introduced the **lambda expression** along with LINQ. The lambda expression is a shorter way of representing anonymous methods using special syntax."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "From Anonymous Methods to Lambda Expressions"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Consider the following anonymous method that checks if a student is a teenager:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Anonymous Method",
        "content": "Traditional anonymous method syntax",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "delegate(Student s) { return s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20; };"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The same logic can be expressed as a lambda expression:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Lambda Expression",
        "content": "Equivalent lambda expression",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "s => s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Lambda Expression Evolution"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The lambda expression evolves from the anonymous method by:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "numbered-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Removing the `delegate` keyword and parameter type",
          "Adding the lambda operator `=>`",
          "Removing curly braces, `return`, and semicolon for single expressions",
          "Removing parentheses when there is only one parameter"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
        "id": "lambda-evolution",
        "caption": "Evolution from anonymous method to lambda expression",
        "definition": "graph LR\n    A[delegate(Student s) { return s.Age > 12 }] --> B[Remove delegate keyword]\n    B --> C[s => s.Age > 12]"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Lambda Expression Structure"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "A lambda expression has three parts:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "**Parameter** (`s`) — The input parameter",
          "**Lambda operator** (`=>`) — Separates parameters from body",
          "**Body expression** (`s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20`) — The logic to execute"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Lambda Expression Variations"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Multiple Parameters"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Wrap parameters in parentheses when you need to pass more than one parameter:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Multiple Parameters",
        "content": "Lambda with multiple parameters",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "(s, youngAge) => s.Age >= youngAge;\n\n// With explicit types\n(Student s, int youngAge) => s.Age >= youngAge;"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 3,
        "text": "No Parameters"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "A lambda expression can be specified without any parameters:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "No Parameters",
        "content": "Lambda without parameters",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "() => Console.WriteLine(\"Parameter less lambda expression\");"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Multiple Statements"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Wrap expressions in curly braces to have more than one statement in the body:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Multiple Statements",
        "content": "Lambda with multiple statements",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "(s, youngAge) =>\n{\n    Console.WriteLine(\"Lambda expression with multiple statements in the body\");\n    return s.Age >= youngAge;\n}"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Local Variables"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "You can declare variables in the expression body:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Local Variables",
        "content": "Lambda with local variable",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "s =>\n{\n    int youngAge = 18;\n    Console.WriteLine(\"Lambda expression with multiple statements in the body\");\n    return s.Age >= youngAge;\n}"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Assigning Lambda to Delegates"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Lambda expressions can be assigned to built-in delegates such as `Func<>`, `Action<>`, and `Predicate<>`."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Func Delegate"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Func<in T, out TResult>` delegate has the return type as the last parameter:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Func Delegate",
        "content": "Lambda assigned to Func delegate",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "Func<Student, bool> isStudentTeenAger = s => s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20;\n\nStudent std = new Student() { Age = 21 };\nbool isTeen = isStudentTeenAger(std); // returns false"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Action Delegate"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Use the `Action` delegate when you don't need to return a value:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Action Delegate",
        "content": "Lambda assigned to Action delegate",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "Action<Student> printStudentDetail = s => Console.WriteLine($\"Name: {s.StudentName}, Age: {s.Age}\");\n\nStudent std = new Student() { StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 21 };\nprintStudentDetail(std);\n// Output: Name: Bill, Age: 21"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Lambda Expressions in LINQ"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Lambda expressions are used extensively with LINQ. The `Where` extension method accepts `Func<TSource, bool>`:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Lambda in LINQ",
        "content": "Using lambda expressions with LINQ",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() { /* ... */ };\n\n// Assign lambda to Func delegate\nFunc<Student, bool> isStudentTeenAger = s => s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20;\n\n// Use in method syntax\nvar teenStudents = studentList.Where(isStudentTeenAger).ToList();\n\n// Use in query syntax\nvar teenStudents2 = from s in studentList\n                    where isStudentTeenAger(s)\n                    select s;"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "key-terms",
      "data": {
        "terms": [
          {
            "term": "Lambda Expression",
            "definition": "A concise way to represent anonymous methods using the => operator."
          },
          {
            "term": "Func<T, TResult>",
            "definition": "A delegate that takes input parameters and returns a value. The last type parameter is the return type."
          },
          {
            "term": "Action<T>",
            "definition": "A delegate that takes input parameters but does not return a value."
          },
          {
            "term": "Predicate<T>",
            "definition": "A delegate that takes a parameter and returns a boolean value."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Lambda expressions are concise anonymous methods using the => operator",
          "They can have zero, one, or multiple parameters",
          "Multiple statements require curly braces and explicit return",
          "Lambda expressions can be assigned to Func, Action, and Predicate delegates",
          "They are the foundation of LINQ method syntax"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next module to learn filtering and projection operators."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "linq-query-syntax",
    "linq-method-syntax"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-006",
  "slug": "linq-method-syntax",
  "moduleSlug": "linq-fundamentals-syntax",
  "title": "LINQ Method Syntax",
  "description": "Learn the fluent method syntax for writing LINQ queries using extension methods.",
  "order": 6,
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedMinutes": 15,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "method syntax",
    "fluent syntax",
    "extension methods"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Method syntax (also known as **fluent syntax**) uses extension methods included in the `Enumerable` or `Queryable` static class, similar to how you would call any extension method of a class."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "info",
        "title": "Compilation",
        "text": "The compiler converts query syntax into method syntax at compile time. Both syntaxes produce the same result."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Method Syntax Basics"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Here is a sample LINQ method syntax query that returns a collection of strings containing the word \"Tutorials\":"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Basic Method Syntax",
        "content": "Filtering strings using method syntax",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "// String collection\nIList<string> stringList = new List<string>() {\n    \"C# Tutorials\",\n    \"VB.NET Tutorials\",\n    \"Learn C++\",\n    \"MVC Tutorials\",\n    \"Java\"\n};\n\n// LINQ Method Syntax\nvar result = stringList.Where(s => s.Contains(\"Tutorials\"));\n\n// Result: \"C# Tutorials\", \"VB.NET Tutorials\", \"MVC Tutorials\""
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Method syntax comprises extension methods and lambda expressions. The `Where()` extension method is defined in the `Enumerable` class."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Understanding the Where Method"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "If you check the signature of the `Where` extension method, you will find that it accepts a **predicate delegate** as `Func<TSource, bool>`. This means you can pass any delegate function that accepts an input parameter and returns a Boolean value."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
        "id": "linq-method-syntax-structure",
        "caption": "Structure of LINQ method syntax",
        "definition": "graph LR\n    A[Collection] --> B[Extension Method]\n    B --> C[Lambda Expression]\n    C --> D[Predicate Delegate]\n    D --> E[Filtered Result]"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The lambda expression works as a delegate passed to the `Where` clause. For example:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Lambda in Where Method",
        "content": "The lambda expression serves as the predicate",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "// The Where method signature:\n// IEnumerable<TSource> Where<TSource>(this IEnumerable<TSource> source, Func<TSource, bool> predicate)\n\n// The lambda expression 's => s.Contains(\"Tutorials\")' matches Func<string, bool>\nvar result = stringList.Where(s => s.Contains(\"Tutorials\"));"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Method Syntax with Objects"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The following example shows how to use LINQ method syntax with an `IEnumerable<T>` collection:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Method Syntax with Objects",
        "content": "Finding teenage students using method syntax",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "// Student collection\nIList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 13 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Moin\", Age = 21 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 20 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 15 }\n};\n\n// LINQ Method Syntax to find teenager students\nvar teenAgerStudents = studentList.Where(s => s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20)\n                                  .ToList<Student>();"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Chaining Methods"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "One of the advantages of method syntax is the ability to chain multiple operations together in a single statement:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Method Chaining",
        "content": "Chaining multiple LINQ operations",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "// Chain Where, OrderBy, and Select\nvar result = studentList\n    .Where(s => s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20)\n    .OrderBy(s => s.StudentName)\n    .Select(s => s.StudentName)\n    .ToList();"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Query Syntax vs Method Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "data": {
        "headers": [
          "Query Syntax",
          "Method Syntax"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "SQL-like syntax",
            "Fluent syntax with extension methods"
          ],
          [
            "Starts with 'from'",
            "Starts with collection name"
          ],
          [
            "Ends with 'select'",
            "Ends with result method like ToList()"
          ],
          [
            "Good for complex queries",
            "Good for simple queries and chaining"
          ],
          [
            "Some operators not supported",
            "Supports all LINQ operators"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Method syntax uses extension methods and lambda expressions",
          "The compiler converts query syntax to method syntax at compile time",
          "Method syntax allows chaining multiple operations",
          "Some LINQ operators are only available in method syntax"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to master lambda expressions, the building blocks of LINQ."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "linq-query-syntax",
    "anatomy-of-lambda-expression"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-005",
  "slug": "linq-query-syntax",
  "moduleSlug": "linq-fundamentals-syntax",
  "title": "LINQ Query Expression Syntax",
  "description": "Learn the SQL-like query syntax for writing LINQ queries in C#.",
  "order": 5,
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedMinutes": 15,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "query syntax",
    "query expression",
    "sql-like"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "There are two basic ways to write a LINQ query for `IEnumerable` collections or `IQueryable` data sources:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "**Query Syntax** (Query Expression Syntax) — SQL-like syntax",
          "**Method Syntax** (Method Extension Syntax / Fluent) — Extension methods"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Query Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Query syntax is similar to SQL (Structured Query Language) used for databases. It is defined directly within C# code."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Query Syntax Structure",
        "content": "The basic structure of a LINQ query",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "from <range variable> in <collection>\n<Standard Query Operators> <lambda expression>\n<select or groupBy operator> <result formation>"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The LINQ query syntax starts with the `from` keyword and ends with the `select` keyword. Here is a sample LINQ query that returns a collection of strings containing the word \"Tutorials\":"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Basic Query Syntax Example",
        "content": "Filtering strings using query syntax",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "// String collection\nIList<string> stringList = new List<string>() {\n    \"C# Tutorials\",\n    \"VB.NET Tutorials\",\n    \"Learn C++\",\n    \"MVC Tutorials\",\n    \"Java\"\n};\n\n// LINQ Query Syntax\nvar result = from s in stringList\n             where s.Contains(\"Tutorials\")\n             select s;\n\n// Result: \"C# Tutorials\", \"VB.NET Tutorials\", \"MVC Tutorials\""
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Query Syntax Structure"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
        "id": "linq-query-structure",
        "caption": "Structure of a LINQ query expression",
        "definition": "graph LR\n    A[from clause] --> B[Range variable]\n    B --> C[where clause]\n    C --> D[Filter condition]\n    D --> E[select clause]\n    E --> F[Result shaping]"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Query syntax starts with a `from` clause followed by a range variable. The `from` clause is structured like `from rangeVariableName in IEnumerableCollection`. In English, this means \"from each object in the collection\". It is similar to a `foreach` loop: `foreach(Student s in studentList)`."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "After the `from` clause, you can use different Standard Query Operators to filter, group, or join elements of the collection. There are around 50 Standard Query Operators available in LINQ."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "LINQ query syntax always ends with a `Select` or `Group` clause. The `Select` clause is used to shape the data. You can select the whole object as it is or only some properties of it."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Query Syntax with Objects"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "In the following example, we use LINQ query syntax to find teenage students from a Student collection:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Query Syntax with Objects",
        "content": "Finding teenage students using query syntax",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "// Student collection\nIList<Student> studentList = new List<Student>() {\n    new Student() { StudentID = 1, StudentName = \"John\", Age = 13 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 2, StudentName = \"Moin\", Age = 21 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 3, StudentName = \"Bill\", Age = 18 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 4, StudentName = \"Ram\", Age = 20 },\n    new Student() { StudentID = 5, StudentName = \"Ron\", Age = 15 }\n};\n\n// LINQ Query Syntax to find teenager students\nvar teenAgerStudent = from s in studentList\n                      where s.Age > 12 && s.Age < 20\n                      select s;"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "key-terms",
      "data": {
        "terms": [
          {
            "term": "Range Variable",
            "definition": "The variable in the from clause that represents each element in the collection, similar to the iteration variable in a foreach loop."
          },
          {
            "term": "from clause",
            "definition": "Specifies the data source and range variable. Must be the first clause in a query expression."
          },
          {
            "term": "where clause",
            "definition": "Filters elements based on a condition. Similar to SQL WHERE."
          },
          {
            "term": "select clause",
            "definition": "Shapes the result data. Must be the last clause in a query expression."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Query syntax is similar to SQL and is defined within C# code",
          "Queries start with 'from' and end with 'select' or 'group by'",
          "The range variable represents each element in the collection",
          "Query syntax is converted to method syntax at compile time"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to learn the method syntax (fluent syntax) for LINQ queries."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "linq-method-syntax",
