{
  "id": "lesson-linq-001",
  "slug": "what-is-linq",
  "moduleSlug": "getting-started-linq",
  "title": "What is LINQ?",
  "description": "Learn what LINQ is, its purpose, and how it provides a unified query experience in C#.",
  "order": 1,
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedMinutes": 15,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "what is linq",
    "language integrated query",
    "introduction"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "**Language-Integrated Query (LINQ)** is a powerful set of technologies based on the integration of query capabilities directly into the C# language. LINQ queries are first-class language constructs in C# .NET, just like classes, methods, and events."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "LINQ provides a consistent query experience to query objects (**LINQ to Objects**), relational databases (**LINQ to SQL**), and XML (**LINQ to XML**)."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Unified Query Syntax"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "LINQ is a uniform query syntax in C# and VB.NET to retrieve data from different sources and formats. It is integrated directly into C#, thereby eliminating the mismatch between programming languages and databases, as well as providing a single querying interface for different types of data sources."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "For example, SQL is a Structured Query Language used to save and retrieve data from a database. In the same way, LINQ is a structured query syntax built into C# and VB.NET to retrieve data from different types of data sources such as collections, ADO.NET DataSet, XML documents, web services, and MS SQL Server."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
        "id": "linq-data-sources",
        "caption": "LINQ can query multiple data sources with the same syntax",
        "definition": "graph LR\n    A[LINQ Query Syntax] --> B[LINQ to Objects]\n    A --> C[LINQ to SQL]\n    A --> D[LINQ to XML]\n    A --> E[LINQ to Entities]\n    B --> F[Collections & Arrays]\n    C --> G[Databases]\n    D --> H[XML Documents]\n    E --> I[Entity Framework]"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Object-Oriented Results"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "LINQ queries return results as **objects**. This enables you to use an object-oriented approach on the result set without worrying about transforming different formats of results into objects."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Your First LINQ Query"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The following example demonstrates a simple LINQ query that retrieves all strings from an array that contain the letter 'a'."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Basic LINQ Query",
        "content": "A simple LINQ query filtering names containing 'a'",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "// Data source\nstring[] names = { \"Bill\", \"Steve\", \"James\", \"Mohan\" };\n\n// LINQ Query\nvar myLinqQuery = from name in names\n                  where name.Contains('a')\n                  select name;\n\n// Query execution\nforeach (var name in myLinqQuery)\n    Console.Write(name + \" \");\n// Output: James Mohan"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 3,
        "text": "How LINQ Queries Execute"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "In the example above, the string array `names` is the data source. The LINQ query is assigned to a variable `myLinqQuery`. You do not get the result of a LINQ query until you execute it."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "LINQ queries can be executed in multiple ways. In this example, we use a `foreach` loop to execute the query stored in `myLinqQuery`. The `foreach` loop executes the query on the data source and retrieves the result, then iterates over the result set."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "info",
        "title": "Important",
        "text": "Every LINQ query must query some kind of data source, whether it is an array, collection, XML, or database. After writing a LINQ query, it must be executed to get the result."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "LINQ Query Structure"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The example query uses the **query syntax** of LINQ. Every LINQ query consists of three main parts:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "**Data source** — The collection, array, or database to query from",
          "**Query** — The filtering, sorting, and projection operations",
          "**Execution** — The mechanism to actually run the query and get results"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "LINQ integrates query capabilities directly into C#",
          "LINQ provides a unified syntax for querying objects, databases, and XML",
          "LINQ queries return results as objects",
          "Queries must be executed (e.g., via foreach) to get results"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to understand why LINQ was created and its advantages over traditional approaches."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "why-linq",
    "linq-api-in-net"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-032",
  "slug": "skip-skipwhile",
  "moduleSlug": "partitioning-operators",
  "title": "Skip & SkipWhile",
  "description": "Learn to skip elements in collections using Skip and SkipWhile operators.",
  "order": 32,
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedMinutes": 15,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "skip",
    "skipwhile",
    "partitioning"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Partitioning operators split a sequence (collection) into two parts and return one of the parts. The `Skip` and `SkipWhile` operators skip elements and return the rest."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Skip Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Skip()` method skips the specified number of elements starting from the first element and returns the rest of the elements."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Skip Example",
        "content": "Skipping the first two elements",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<string> strList = new List<string>() { \"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\", \"Four\", \"Five\" };\n\nvar newList = strList.Skip(2);\n\n// Result: \"Three\", \"Four\", \"Five\""
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "SkipWhile Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `SkipWhile()` method skips elements as long as the specified condition is true. Once the condition becomes false, it returns all remaining elements."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "SkipWhile Example",
        "content": "Skipping elements while condition is true",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<string> strList = new List<string>() { \"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\", \"Four\", \"Five\", \"Six\" };\n\nvar resultList = strList.SkipWhile(s => s.Length < 4);\n\n// Result: \"Three\", \"Four\", \"Five\", \"Six\"\n// Skips \"One\" and \"Two\" (length < 4), then returns rest"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "SkipWhile with No Skipping",
        "content": "When first element doesn't satisfy condition",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<string> strList = new List<string>() { \"Three\", \"One\", \"Two\", \"Four\", \"Five\", \"Six\" };\n\nvar resultList = strList.SkipWhile(s => s.Length < 4);\n\n// Result: \"Three\", \"One\", \"Two\", \"Four\", \"Five\", \"Six\"\n// No elements skipped because \"Three\" (length 5) doesn't satisfy condition"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "SkipWhile with Index"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The second overload of `SkipWhile` includes the index of each element:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "SkipWhile with Index",
        "content": "Skipping based on element and index",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<string> strList = new List<string>() { \"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\", \"Four\", \"Five\", \"Six\" };\n\nvar result = strList.SkipWhile((s, i) => s.Length > i);\n\n// Result: \"Five\", \"Six\"\n// Skips elements where length > index"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "warning",
        "title": "Not Supported in Query Syntax",
        "text": "Skip and SkipWhile are not supported in C# query syntax. Use method syntax only."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Skip skips a specified number of elements from the start",
          "SkipWhile skips elements while condition is true",
          "Once SkipWhile condition becomes false, returns all remaining elements",
          "Both support index-based overloads"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next lesson to learn about Take and TakeWhile."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "take-takewhile"
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lesson-linq-033",
  "slug": "take-takewhile",
  "moduleSlug": "partitioning-operators",
  "title": "Take & TakeWhile",
  "description": "Learn to take elements from collections using Take and TakeWhile operators.",
  "order": 33,
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedMinutes": 15,
  "tags": [
    "linq",
    "take",
    "takewhile",
    "partitioning"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Take` and `TakeWhile` operators return a specified number of elements from the start of a collection."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Take Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `Take()` method returns the specified number of elements starting from the first element."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Take Example",
        "content": "Taking the first two elements",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<string> strList = new List<string>() { \"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\", \"Four\", \"Five\" };\n\nvar newList = strList.Take(2);\n\n// Result: \"One\", \"Two\""
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "TakeWhile Operator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The `TakeWhile()` method returns elements as long as the specified condition is true. Once the condition becomes false, it stops returning elements."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "TakeWhile Example",
        "content": "Taking elements while condition is true",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<string> strList = new List<string>() { \"Three\", \"Four\", \"Five\", \"Hundred\" };\n\nvar result = strList.TakeWhile(s => s.Length > 4);\n\n// Result: \"Three\"\n// Only first element has length > 4"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "TakeWhile with Index"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The second overload of `TakeWhile` includes the index of each element:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "TakeWhile with Index",
        "content": "Taking based on element and index",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "IList<string> strList = new List<string>() { \"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\", \"Four\", \"Five\", \"Six\" };\n\nvar resultList = strList.TakeWhile((s, i) => s.Length > i);\n\n// Result: \"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\", \"Four\""
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "warning",
        "title": "Not Supported in Query Syntax",
        "text": "Take and TakeWhile are not supported in C# query syntax. Use method syntax only."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Paging with Skip and Take"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "A common use case for Skip and Take is implementing paging:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Paging Example",
        "content": "Implementing paging with Skip and Take",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "int pageNumber = 2;\nint pageSize = 10;\n\nvar page = students\n    .Skip((pageNumber - 1) * pageSize)\n    .Take(pageSize)\n    .ToList();"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Take returns a specified number of elements from the start",
          "TakeWhile returns elements while condition is true",
          "Once TakeWhile condition becomes false, stops returning elements",
          "Commonly used with Skip for paging"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to the next module to learn about conversion and generation operators."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "skip-skipwhile"
  ]
}
